            <ArticleTitle>An update on the role of opioids in the management of chronic pain of nonmalignant origin.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>451-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE OF REVIEW" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To summarize and reflect over primarily recent epidemiological and randomized controlled trials in opioid-treated chronic nonmalignant pain patients, focusing on effects, side effects, risks and long-term consequences of the treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RECENT FINDINGS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In the western world opioids are increasingly being used for long-term treatment of chronic nonmalignant pain. While the long-term benefits of opioids regarding pain relief, functional capacity and health-related quality of life still remain to be proven, studies are emerging that describe serious long-term consequences such as addiction, opioid-induced hyperalgesia, cognitive disorders, and suppression of the immune and reproductive systems.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Much more research is needed concerning long-time effects and consequences of opioid therapy in chronic nonmalignant pain patients; however, some clear warning signals have been sent out within recent years.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
